\begin{question}
\textbf{Continuous random variables}

Which of the following random variables is a \emph{continuous} random variable?
\begin{answerlist}
\item The time (in minutes) until the next customer enters a shop.
\item The number of customers who enter a shop within one hour.
\item The number of defective items in a delivery of 100 items.
\item The payment method $\in\{\texttt{cash}, \texttt{card}\}$ of the next customer.
\item The result of rolling a die.
\end{answerlist}
\end{question}

\begin{solution}
\begin{answerlist}
\item True. Time can take any value in an interval, e.g., $[0,\infty)$.
\item False. A count $\in\{0,1,2,\dots\}$ is discrete.
\item False. A count $\in\{0,1,\dots,100\}$ is discrete.
\item False. Only two possible values: discrete (and not even numeric).
\item False. Only the six values $1,\dots,6$: discrete.
\end{answerlist}
\end{solution}

